# NativMix Bug / Issue Triage

When discussing a **new bug, GitHub issue, or user report**, follow this order. Do not jump straight to coding unless the user explicitly asks to implement now.

## 1. Relevant code first

- Locate the platform/backend path (e.g. PipeWire vs WASAPI, GUI vs worker).
- Compare with the working equivalent if one exists (Linux vs Windows).
- Summarize briefly for the maintainer: what runs, what is missing, why the report fits.

## 2. Lock a battle plan (before any reply)

- Agree scope with the maintainer: fix / defer / won’t-fix / close — per bullet if the report has a list.
- Write or update a short plan under `.cursor/plans/` when more than one issue or bullet is in play.
- **Freeze:** once the maintainer confirms the plan, treat that disposition as locked for drafts and posts.
- If the maintainer later changes scope: **update the plan first**, then regenerate reply drafts — do not invent a new “won’t fix / will fix” mid-air without that lock.
- Platform-only fixes (e.g. Windows WASAPI): prefer a **dedicated branch / test build** so Linux users are not flooded with releases while iterating; merge after reporter smoke-test when practical.

## 3. Formulate the reply (only after the plan is locked)

- Draft a paste-ready comment (usually English on GitHub) that matches the **locked** plan — no extras, no speculative promises.
- Acknowledge the report; state what you found in plain language (no deep code dump unless asked).
- Point at existing wiki when the feature already is documented; do not promise a wiki pass “to clarify” unless the plan says so.
- Keep log/detail requests light unless the maintainer wants them.
- **Do not post** to GitHub until the maintainer explicitly asks to reply / post (draft ≠ publish).

## 4. Then proceed as planned

- Only after steps 1–3 and maintainer go-ahead: implement, branch, tests, wiki/changelog — whatever was agreed.
- Do not treat the reply draft as “done” for the codebase until the planned work is actually started or explicitly deferred with a plan note.

## Anti-patterns

- Coding or posting before a locked plan and (for GH) an explicit “post this” from the maintainer.
- Flipping fix/defer/won’t-fix in the reply while the plan still says something else — or rewriting the reply three times before locking scope.
- Spamming main/release channels for exploratory platform fixes.
- Asking the reporter for noise (logs, hardware path) when the code gap is already clear.
